{"id": "8c6c5f91-2b53-44c5-9d05-dc464e09d7d0", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T14:32:00.152423", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "4260135b-7027-4a6d-80ee-0adc0d8b3a33", "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-e70f1856d0b2422da043e6bca4409323", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"phase\": \"exploration\", \"command\": \"ls -la / 2>/dev/null; echo \\\"---tmp---\\\"; ls -la /tmp 2>/dev/null | head -20; echo \\\"---net---\\\"; python3 -c \\\"import socket; socket.create_connection(('pypi.org',443),timeout=2)\\\" 2>&1 | head -2\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.116, \"output\": \"total 88\\ndrwxr-xr-x   12 154547 30      240 Oct 22 21:32 .\\ndrwxr-xr-x   12 154547 30      240 Oct 22 21:32 ..\\ndrwxr-xr-x    2 65534  65534 69632 Oct 22 07:19 bin\\ndrwxr-xr-x    4 154547 30      320 Oct 22 21:32 dev\\ndrwx------    2 154547 30       60 Oct 22 21:32 etc\\ndrwxr-xr-x    3 154547 30       60 Oct 22 21:32 home\\ndrwxr-xr-x  113 65534  65534  4096 Jul 15 16:57 lib\\ndrwxr-xr-x    2 65534  65534  4096 Apr 23  2026 lib64\\ndr-xr-xr-x 3253 65534  65534     0 Oct 22 21:32 proc\\ndrwxr-xr-x    2 154547 30       40 Oct 22 21:32 tmp\\ndrwxr-xr-x   14 65534  65534  4096 Jun  4 17:35 usr\\ndrwx------    2 154547 30     4096 Oct 22 21:31 workspace\\n---tmp---\\ntotal 0\\ndrwxr-xr-x  2 154547 30  40 Oct 22 21:32 .\\ndrwxr-xr-x 12 154547 30 240 Oct 22 21:32 ..\\n---net---\\nTraceback (most recent call last):\\n  File \\\"<string>\\\", line 1, in <module>\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "98bb4732-53ca-4a05-8e6b-85e9cfdd36a1", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent"}
{"id": "c6bf0c8e-d1ec-4c9a-9c12-fde544b0fbb8", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T14:32:55.452757", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "8c6c5f91-2b53-44c5-9d05-dc464e09d7d0", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "I have completed the inspection. The workspace is essentially empty — this is a pure design-only task. Let me now deliver the three requested items as a plan-stage handoff.\n\n"}], "reasoning_content": "The environment is:\n- Python 3.14.4 system Python, no numpy/scipy/matplotlib/mdtraj/MDAnalysis installed\n- No MDGEN code, no data, no track.md file (only the task.json references it as a note)\n- Network is disabled (pypi connection failed)\n- GPU disabled\n- 224 CPUs\n\nSo the design must be a pure design-only deliverable. The design can rely on nothing concrete from the repository — no MDGEN code, no trajectories, no data formats. This is a \"design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"\n\nNow I need to deliver the researcher_request's three items:\n1) Input/repository inspection — what's actually present\n2) Proposed approach (sketch)\n3) Consequential assumptions/uncertainties\n\nLet me think about the actual science of this design since I'll need a strong approach sketch.\n\nThe question: Does the MDGEN model accurately recover fast timestep dynamics (e.g., oscillations in torsion angles) that are missing in subsampled molecular dynamics trajectories?\n\nContext: MDGEN — this refers to the Google DeepMind paper \"MDGen: A Unified Framework for Molecular Dynamics Generation\" (June 2025), a generative model (rectified flow) trained on MD17 / TrajectoryNet datasets, which generates full molecular dynamics trajectories. A key finding in that paper is that MDGEN trained on subsampled trajectories (e.g., 50 ps) fails to reproduce fast oscillations — the paper reports that models trained on time-subsampled MD trajectories produce \"unphysical dynamics,\" specifically missing high-frequency torsional oscillations, resulting in flattened power spectra / autocorrelation functions, and that MDGEN restores these when trained on full trajectories but degrades under subsampling.\n\nActually the paper's key observation: when training trajectory generators on MD data subsampled in time (e.g., every N ps), the model misses the fast modes — torsion angle autocorrelation decays much slower / the power spectrum lacks high-frequency peaks. MDGEN trained on full trajectory data can generate trajectories whose torsion-angle autocorrelation functions match reference.\n\nThe design should compare:\n- Reference high-frequency trajectories (full-resolution MD, e.g., MD17 aspirin at 0.5 fs timestep)\n- Subsample reference at various intervals (e.g., 10 fs, 100 fs, 1 ps, 5 ps, 10 ps) to produce training data\n- Train MDGEN variants on each subsampled dataset (or use the pretrained checkpoints if available)\n- Generate trajectories from MDGEN\n- Compare the generated trajectories against:\n  - full-resolution reference (ground truth fast dynamics)\n  - the subsampled reference (what the generator could have seen)\n\nMetrics for fast-timescale recovery:\n1. Torsion-angle autocorrelation function (TACF): C(τ) = <cos(φ(t+τ) − φ(t))> or normalized autocorrelation of φ(t). Compare decay time constant / integral correlation time between generated and reference.\n2. Power spectral density (PSD) of torsion angles: Fourier transform of TACF; look for high-frequency peaks (e.g., for aspirin, methyl or carboxyl torsion modes at frequencies corresponding to periods tens of fs). Check whether generated PSD shows the high-frequency peaks or is flattened.\n3. Distribution of torsion angles (dihedral probability density), especially checking for symmetric/oscillatory modes.\n4. RMSD of TACF or PSD between generated and reference as a scalar score.\n5. Correlation time τ_int = integral of normalized TACF — a single scalar comparing \"how fast dynamics decay.\"\n6. Comparison to subsampling: the \"upper bound\" for what a pure interpolator could recover. The reference for the question is: the subsampled trajectory literally lacks the fast oscillations (aliasing). So a control: show that naive interpolation / the training data itself has flattened TACF.\n\nControls:\n- Same generated-trajectory length and number of independent generations (seeds) for statistical comparison; multiple seeds with error bars.\n- Compare MDGEN output trained on full-trajectory vs. subsampled — this isolates the \"subsampling\" cause.\n- Compare generated vs. subsampled reference: if generated matches the subsampled reference but not the full reference, the model learned the subsampled distribution; if it matches full reference, it recovered dynamics beyond training data (unlikely for true missing information but tests whether the model generalizes / prior helps).\n- Baseline: a \"copy subsampled\" or linear/deterministic interpolation baseline to quantify what information is fundamentally absent and what an interpolation baseline achieves.\n- Check that the fast modes exist in the reference at all: verify reference TACF/PSD has peaks above the subsampling Nyquist frequency.\n- Thermodynamic/static checks as negative controls: energy distributions, radial distribution functions, equilibrium torsion distributions — these should still be accurate under subsampling (subsampling doesn't destroy the Boltzmann distribution), confirming that failure to recover TACF is specifically about temporal dynamics, not static equilibrium. This is important to isolate \"fast dynamics\" from \"equilibrium structure.\"\n- Stability/validity check: generated structures should be physically valid (no clashes, reasonable energies) — to rule out that dynamics differences stem from broken structures.\n- Runtime budget: design should account for CPU-only (no GPU), so either use pretrained MDGEN checkpoints (not available locally) or small systems.\n\nNow the important constraint: The environment has NO MDGEN code, NO data, NO network, NO GPU. So the honest deliverable is: all of this must be treated as assumptions — the design relies on obtaining MDGEN checkpoints/code and reference MD trajectories, which are not present in the workspace. The design must state how those would be sourced (e.g., DeepMind's official checkpoints + TrajectoryNet/MD17 data) and that these are outside the sandbox.\n\nI should be careful not to fabricate: I must not claim MDGEN exists at a certain URL as fact verified here; I can propose it as an assumption. Actually I know MDGEN is a real published model (arXiv 2506.xxxx, \"MDGen: A Unified Framework for Molecular Dynamics Generation\"), but I should frame external sourcing as an assumption since I cannot verify in the sandbox.\n\nLet me now craft the three deliverables:\n\n1) Input/repository inspection:\n- /workspace contains only task.json\n- task.json includes a \"documents\" entry with track.md whose content is literally: \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"\n- No MDGEN code, no model checkpoints, no trajectory data, no documentation files, no notebooks, no configs.\n- Environment: Python 3.14.4 system; numpy, scipy, matplotlib, mdtraj, MDAnalysis NOT installed; network disabled (pypi unreachable); no GPU; 224 CPUs.\n- Implication: The design cannot rely on any in-workspace artifact; all model/data specifics must be stated assumptions, and \"computability\" in this sandbox is not a requirement per the task note.\n\n2) Proposed approach sketch:\n- Phase A: Data construction. Use one small, well-understood system (e.g., a single small molecule like aspirin, as in MD17-style) with a full-resolution reference trajectory at a timestep short enough to resolve fast torsional oscillations (e.g., 0.5 fs, trajectory of ~50–100 ps). \n- Phase B: Subsampling. Produce K subsampled variants (e.g., keep every 10 fs, 50 fs, 100 fs, 500 fs, 1 ps, 5 ps, 10 ps) → these represent \"subsampled MD\" that miss/render as aliased the fast torsion oscillations (high freq beyond Nyquist).\n- Phase C: Model. Obtain/train MDGEN variants: (i) trained on full-resolution data, (ii) trained on each subsampled variant. Generate many trajectories (identical lengths, multiple seeds).\n- Phase D: Metrics. \n  (a) Torsion-angle autocorrelation function C(t) per rotatable dihedral; \n  (b) integral correlation time τ_int; \n  (c) power spectral density via FFT of C(t), detection of high-frequency peaks and their amplitude vs. baseline noise; \n  (d) full dihedral distribution (static) to separate equilibrium from dynamics; \n  (e) scalar discrepancy metrics: RMSE/relative L2 between reference and generated C(t) and PSD, computed per-dihedral and aggregated.\n  Report: does the generated PSD/TACF recover peaks above the subsampling Nyquist? Compare against the subsampled reference's own TACF/PSD to quantify what's \"missing.\"\n- Phase E: Controls.\n  - Nyquist control: verify fast modes exist in reference above each subsample Nyquist frequency (reference PSD peak present).\n  - Static-vs-dynamic control: compare equilibrium dihedral distributions & RDFs; expectation that subsampling preserves equilibrium but destroys TACF — isolating that the question is about temporal dynamics.\n  - Baseline: naive interpolation (linear in Cartesian or torsion space) of the subsampled trajectory as a lower bound \"what you can recover from the subsampled data alone.\"\n  - Statistical: multiple seeds, bootstrap CIs on τ_int and PSD peaks; report means ± CI.\n  - Validity check: potential energy / closest-contact sanity on generated frames.\n- Answer structure: define \"accurate recovery\" a priori — e.g., generated C(τ) within a tolerance band of reference and/or presence of reference high-freq peaks in generated PSD at non-trivial amplitude; otherwise \"no.\"\n\n3) Consequential assumptions/uncertainties:\n- Assumption: MDGEN refers to the published generative MD trajectory model with available reference implementation/checkpoints; obtainable external to sandbox (network/sandbox provides none).\n- Assumption: reference trajectories are MD17/TrajectoryNet-style all-atom MD at 0.5 fs, with explicit solvent absent (vacuum) or as in source dataset; torsion-angle dynamics are resolvable at that resolution.\n- Uncertainty: \"accurate recovery\" threshold — propose pre-registered tolerance (e.g., relative L2 on C(t) ≤ 0.1, or recovered peak amplitude ≥ 50% of reference) — set by design, defensible.\n- Uncertainty/assumption: runtime budget — no GPU; training 7 MDGEN variants CPU-only may be prohibitive; mitigation: use pretrained checkpoints for inference, or reduce to fewer subsample levels / rely on a single small molecule.\n- Assumption: definition of \"fast timestep dynamics\" = torsion-angle oscillation modes above the subsampling Nyquist frequency; oscillations defined via TACF/PSD.\n- Assumption: how MDGEN was trained (conditioning, number of steps, rectified flow) — use official defaults; treat as fixed.\n- Uncertainties: whether the model restores fast dynamics beyond what training data shows is an empirical outcome — not known a priori; the design must be capable of both outcomes.\n- Metric definition details: TACF normalization, windowing for PSD, per-dihedral weighting (e.g., methyl groups vs. heavy torsions) — state explicitly.\n\nLet me also note that I shouldn't claim a specific expected outcome as verified. Good to note that the design's conclusion criterion must be pre-declared.\n\nLet me write the plan handoff. The researcher wants me to only deliver plan stage now. I'll respond with end_researcher_turn stage=plan answering the three items.\n\nLet me structure the answer clearly. Keep it thorough but not bloated.", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "## 1) Input/repository inspection (what is actually present)\n\n**Workspace contents:** `/workspace` contains exactly one file, `task.json`. There are no other files, no hidden files, no subdirectories, no code, no checkpoints, and no data anywhere under `/workspace`, `/tmp`, or the container root that I could find.\n\n`task.json` content:\n- question: \"Does the MDGEN model accurately recover the fast timestep dynamics (e.g., oscillations in torsion angles) that are missing in subsampled molecular dynamics trajectories?\"\n- context: \"Design an experiment that answers the question... Do not claim that an experiment was run.\"\n- documents.track.md = \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"\n\n**Environment facts (verified):**\n- Python 3.14.4 (system); **none** of numpy, scipy, matplotlib, mdtraj, or MDAnalysis are importable (all failed).\n- Network is disabled (pypi.org connection failed) — nothing can be downloaded.\n- No GPU; 224 CPUs reported.\n- No `track.md` file exists on disk despite the documents entry — it is only an embedded note.\n\n**Implications for any design:** There is no MDGEN implementation, no model description, no reference trajectory data, and no trajectory file formats present to \"rely on.\" The design therefore must (a) treat all model/data specifics as explicitly stated assumptions sourced from literature/outside the sandbox, and (b) not depend on in-workspace compute artifacts. The task note explicitly says repositories and compute are not required, so a complete, defensible experimental design is the deliverable.\n\n## 2) Proposed approach (sketch — not the full design)\n\n**A. Fixed reference data (ground truth for fast dynamics).**\nChoose one small, well characterized all-atom system where rotatable torsions oscillate at sub-ps periods (MD17/TrajectoryNet-style small molecule, e.g., aspirin or a similar drug-like molecule), with a full-resolution reference MD trajectory at a timestep short enough to resolve the fast modes (e.g., 0.5 fs, total ~50–100 ps, as in the MD17 dataset). This is the \"high-frequency reference\" that subsampled data cannot represent.\n\n**B. Subsampling (the \"missing dynamics\" condition).**\nCreate K subsampled versions of the reference by keeping every Nth frame to mimic typical trajectory storage (e.g., stride that yields 10 fs, 50 fs, 100 fs, 500 fs, 1 ps, 5 ps, 10 ps effective spacing). The fast torsional oscillations (period ~tens of fs) will be under-sampled (below Nyquist) or aliased in these variants — this is the precise operation the question asks about.\n\n**C. Model conditions.**\nMDGEN variants: (i) trained on the full-resolution trajectory, (ii) trained on each subsampled variant, using the same official architecture/training defaults. Generate a fixed count of trajectories of fixed length from each variant across multiple seeds. If model training is not feasible given budget, use the official pretrained checkpoints for inference on the standard dataset (see assumptions). The key contrast is MDGEN trained on subsampled vs. full data, because it isolates whether the missing fast dynamics come from the subsampling of the training signal.\n\n**D. Fast-timescale metrics (candidate set).**\n- **Torsion-angle autocorrelation function (TACF):** per rotatable dihedral, C(τ) = <cos(Δφ(τ))> (or normalized autocorrelation of φ(t)); compute for reference, each subsampled reference, and each MDGEN-generated ensemble.\n- **Integral correlation time** τ_int = ∫₀^∞ C(τ)dτ — a single scalar summarizing decay rate.\n- **Power spectral density (PSD):** FFT of C(t) per dihedral; explicitly test whether high-frequency peaks present in the full reference (above each subsample's Nyquist) appear in the generated PSD, and at what amplitude relative to the reference peak.\n- **Static equilibrium check (isolating \"dynamics\" from \"equilibrium\"):** torsion-angle probability distributions and RDFs — subsampling does not change the Boltzmann distribution, so these should match under all conditions. This confirms any TACF/PSD failure is specifically a temporal-dynamics problem, not broken structure.\n- **Scalar discrepancies:** per-dihedral RMSE / relative-L2 between reference and generated C(τ) and PSD, aggregated with bootstrap confidence intervals.\n- **Validity control:** potential-energy / closest-contact sanity checks on generated frames so dynamics differences cannot be blamed on unphysical structures.\n\n**E. Controls needed.**\n- **Nyquist control:** verify the fast modes actually exist in the reference (reference PSD peak above the subsample Nyquist) before claiming they are \"missing.\"\n- **Baseline interpolation:** naive interpolation (linear in torsion/Cartesian space) of each subsampled trajectory — the lower bound of what any method can recover from the subsampled information alone.\n- **Statistical rigor:** many seeds, bootstrap CIs on τ_int and PSD peak amplitudes.\n- **Pre-registered outcome criterion:** define \"accurate recovery\" before running — e.g., generated C(τ) within a stated tolerance of the full-reference C(τ), and/or recovery of reference high-frequency PSD peaks at ≥ stated fraction of reference amplitude. The design must be capable of answering \"no\" cleanly.\n\n## 3) Consequential assumptions & uncertainties (and how I propose to treat them)\n\n1. **MDGEN provenance.** Assumption: MDGEN is the published generative model for entire MD trajectories with an official reference implementation/checkpoints, obtainable outside this sandbox (workspace provides none; network disabled). Treated as a fixed external input sourced at design time; design does not require building it from scratch.\n2. **Reference data.** Assumption: high-frequency reference is an MD17/TrajectoryNet-style all-atom trajectory at 0.5 fs for a single small drug-like molecule. Treated as a fixed input; exact molecule and force field named at design time.\n3. **\"Accurate recovery\" definition.** Uncertainty — must be made operational. Proposal: pre-register a tolerance (relative-L2 on C(τ) ≤ ~0.1, and/or ≥ ~50% recovery of reference high-freq PSD peak amplitude). Stated as a design decision, not a finding.\n4. **Runtime/compute budget.** Uncertainty: no GPU; training 7 MDGEN variants CPU-only may be prohibitive. Proposal: default to official pretrained checkpoints for inference; if unavailable, reduce scope to the full vs. a single strong subsample (e.g., 1 ps) contrast plus a coarse subsample sweep. Design states both paths.\n5. **Metric definitions.** Uncertainties: TACF normalization, PSD windowing, per-dihedral weighting (methyl vs. heavy torsions), and which torsions count as \"fast.\" Proposal: define each explicitly in the full design (standard normalized TACF, uniform Hamming-windowed FFT, report heavy torsions separately from methyl groups).\n6. **Empirical outcome polarity.** Uncertainty: whether MDGEN can restore dynamics beyond its training signal (or only recover what subsampled data show) is unknown a priori. Treated as an open empirical question the design must adjudicate; the comparison \"generated vs. subsampled reference\" and \"generated vs. full reference\" makes both answers interpretable (recovered beyond training data vs. faithfully reproduced the aliased/smoothed subsampled statistics).\n7. **Formal correctness claim.** I will not claim any experiment ran or any result was observed; only the design is delivered.", "evidence": "Executed inspection: (1) `ls -la /workspace` shows only `task.json`; `find /workspace -type f` confirms no other files, no hidden files, no subdirectories. (2) `cat /workspace/task.json` shows the question/context and the embedded note documents.track.md = \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" (3) Environment checks: Python 3.14.4; imports of numpy, scipy, matplotlib, mdtraj, MDAnalysis all raise ImportError; socket connect to pypi.org:443 fails (network disabled); no GPU. (4) Filesystem scan of /, /tmp shows no MDGEN code, checkpoints, trajectory files, notebooks, configs, or README docs anywhere.", "assumptions": ["MDGEN refers to the published MD-trajectory generative model, sourced (code+checkpoints) 