{"id": "2b9f76b9-11fd-4070-bf34-69bedb99dc43", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T14:33:17.458137", "source": "user", "parent_id": "109cec02-85aa-4f57-b0e8-1b3071696d32", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approved. The workspace inspection (only task.json, no code/data/network) and the proposed approach are sound, and your assumption-handling is appropriate for a design-only task. Please now produce the final experiment design covering the full deliverable: rationale, controls, analysis plan, and feasibility. Lock in these agreed points while writing it:\\n\\n(1) Central contrast = MDGEN trained on full-resolution reference vs. MDGEN trained on each subsampled variant, so the design isolates whether missing fast dynamics stem from the subsampled training signal. Keep the interpolation baseline and the Nyquist control (verify the fast modes exist above the subsample Nyquist in the full reference before claiming they are missing).\\n\\n(2) Make \\\"accurate recovery\\\" pre-registered and directional so a clean \\\"no\\\" is answerable: state explicit tolerances (e.g., relative-L2 on C(tau) and the fraction of reference high-frequency PSD peak amplitude required) chosen before running, with justification.\\n\\n(3) In the analysis plan, explicitly separate the two interpretable outcomes: (a) generated ensemble matches the alias-flattened/smoothed subsampled statistics (faithful reproduction, no extrapolation) vs. (b) generated ensemble matches the full reference's fast modes (recovery beyond the training signal). State that the empirical polarity is an open question the design adjudicates.\\n\\n(4) Keep the equilibrium-control logic (static torsion distributions / RDFs must match under all conditions since subsampling preserves Boltzmann structure) as the check that any TACF/PSD discrepancy is a temporal-dynamics problem, not structural degradation.\\n\\n(5) Include a feasibility section honoring the constraints you verified (no GPU, 224 CPUs, no network/dependencies): inference-first path using official pretrained checkpoints, with a defined fallback reducing the subsample sweep if training is required.\\n\\nDo not claim any experiment was run or any result observed; deliver only the design, with any remaining dataset/model specifics stated as explicit assumptions rather than facts.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "63c1445a-4d5d-48b6-ae0f-8b37ea2554f5", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T14:34:00.579992", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "2b9f76b9-11fd-4070-bf34-69bedb99dc43", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "The plan is approved. Since the workspace/environment is already fully characterized and there is no code or data to inspect further, I'll now deliver the complete final experiment design incorporating all locked-in points.\n\n"}], "reasoning_content": "The researcher approved the plan and gave specific locked-in points. Now I need to produce the final experiment design with full deliverable: rationale, controls, analysis plan, feasibility, and the locked points. Let me write a comprehensive design-only answer with stage=result.\n\nKey components:\n1. Rationale\n2. Fixed inputs / assumptions (explicit, not facts)\n3. Central contrast (full vs. subsampled training)\n4. Subsample levels and Nyquist control\n5. Interpolation baseline\n6. Metrics (TACF, τ_int, PSD, static equilibrium, validity)\n7. Pre-registered \"accurate recovery\" criterion (directional, tolerances with justification)\n8. Analysis plan separating the two interpretable outcomes (a) faithful alias-flattened reproduction vs (b) recovery beyond training signal\n9. Controls\n10. Feasibility (inference-first, fallback to reduced sweep)\n11. Explicit statement: no experiment run, outcome unknown\n\nI don't need more exploration — environment is already fully characterized. Let me write the full design.\n\nLet me be thorough and rigorous. I'll structure it clearly.", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-c8e0592ced2045f69a87ebeac0b9d201", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-c8e0592ced2045f69a87ebeac0b9d201", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"answer\": \"# Final Experiment Design (Design-Only — No Experiment Conducted)\\n\\n## 0. Status statement\\nThis is a design-only deliverable. No experiment was run, no result is reported, and no empirical outcome is claimed. All model/data specifics not verifiable in the sandbox are stated explicitly as assumptions in §7 and are treated as fixed external inputs, not facts.\\n\\n## 1. Research question and objective\\n**Question:** Does the MDGEN model accurately recover the fast timestep dynamics (e.g., torsion-angle oscillations) that are missing in subsampled molecular dynamics trajectories?\\n\\n**Objective:** A controlled, pre-registered comparison that determines whether MDGEN — when trained on MD trajectories that have been subsampled in time (so that fast torsional modes are under-sampled/aliased below the Nyquist frequency) — (a) faithfully reproduces the smoothed/alias-flattened dynamics present in its subsampled training signal, or (b) recovers the fast oscillatory modes present in a full-resolution reference. The design is built so that a clean, directional \\\"no\\\" (outcome (a)) is answerable.\\n\\n## 2. Rationale\\nTorsion-angle oscillations in typical drug-like molecules have periods on the order of tens of femtoseconds. Routine trajectory storage subsamples MD output to 100 fs–10 ps, which is coarser than the Nyquist limit for these modes; the stored trajectory therefore contains alias-flattened, high-frequency-smoothed autocorrelation structure. A generative model such as MDGEN trained on such data has a priori no faithful information about the fast modes in its training signal. The correct way to attribute whatever the generated ensemble shows is to hold the model architecture fixed and vary only the time-subsampling of its training data, contrasting:\\n\\n- **MDGEN(FULL)** — trained on the full-resolution reference (fast modes fully present in training);\\n- **MDGEN(sub_k)** — trained on each of K subsampled variants (fast modes missing/aliased in training).\\n\\nIf MDGEN(sub_k) matches the subsampled reference's flattened statistics rather than the full reference's fast modes, the design answers \\\"no\\\" (no recovery of missing dynamics). If MDGEN(sub_k) matches the full reference's fast modes, the design answers \\\"yes\\\" (recovery beyond the training signal) — an outcome that would require the model to extrapolate/regenerate physically plausible oscillations rather than memorize them. Both outcomes must be adjudicated, not assumed.\\n\\n## 3. Fixed inputs (stated as assumptions, per §7)\\n- **System/reference:** one small, well-characterized all-atom drug-like molecule (MD17/TrajectoryNet-style, e.g., aspirin or equivalent) simulated in vacuum at 0.5 fs timestep, ~50–100 ps total length, with a stored frame every 0.5 fs (full-resolution reference) plus the accompanying topology.\\n- **Model:** the published MDGEN trajectory-generation model with its official architecture, conditioning, and preprocessing defaults (see §7 for provenance).\\n- **Tooling for analysis:** analysis code written against the reference trajectory format; computed with plain Python + standard numerics available in the environment or installed from the pinned requirements at design time (§6).\\n\\n## 4. Experimental conditions and data construction\\n1. **Full-resolution reference** R0: the 0.5 fs trajectory, the ground truth for fast dynamics.\\n2. **Subsampled references** R_k, k = 1..K: keep every Nth frame of R0 to produce effective frame spacings of {10 fs, 50 fs, 100 fs, 500 fs, 1 ps, 5 ps, 10 ps} (K = 7; K is reduced to {1 ps}+coarse levels under the feasibility fallback, §6). These R_k are the \\\"subsampled MD\\\" that the question refers to. The Nyquist frequency of each R_k is f_nyq(k) = 1/(2·spacing_k).\\n3. **Training sets:** one per condition — (i) R0 and (ii) each R_k — prepared exactly as MDGEN expects (same conditioning, normalization, train/validation split).\\n4. **Model conditions:** MDGEN(FULL) trained on R0; MDGEN(sub_k) trained on each R_k.\\n5. **Generation:** from each trained condition, generate a fixed number G of trajectories (suggested G = 100) of fixed length L (e.g., ≥ 5 × the longest correlation time of interest) across multiple independent seeds, using identical decoding-step configuration. All conditions generate the same length so TACF/PSD windows are comparable.\\n\\n## 5. Metrics and analysis plan\\nAll metrics are computed per identifiable rotatable dihedral (defined from the reference topology), with methyl-group torsions reported separately from heavy-atom torsions, because their correlation times differ.\\n\\n**M1 — Torsion-angle autocorrelation function (TACF):** for each dihedral, C(τ) = <cos(φ(t+τ) − φ(t))>, computed on: the full reference R0, each subsampled reference R_k, the interpolation baseline, and each generated ensemble. Normalization is explicit (C(0)=1), and τ windowing is uniform (Hamming) so PSDs are comparable.\\n\\n**M2 — Integral correlation time:** τ_int = ∫ C(τ)dτ (upper cut at first zero crossing). Single scalar per dihedral summarizing decay rate; the primary \\\"how fast is the dynamics\\\" number.\\n\\n**M3 — Power spectral density (PSD):** FFT of each C(τ). For each dihedral and each R_k, flag the reference high-frequency peaks lying above f_nyq(k) — these are the \\\"missing fast modes\\\" for that subsample. Compare the generated PSD's amplitude at those frequencies against the R0 peak amplitude.\\n\\n**M4 — Equilibrium (static) controls:** torsion-angle probability distributions P(φ) and site–site radial distribution functions (RDFs). These must match between R0, R_k, and every generated ensemble within tolerance (§5 controls) — subsampling preserves the Boltzmann distribution, so any TACF/PSD discrepancy while P(φ)/RDF match cleanly isolates the failure to *temporal* dynamics, not structural degradation.\\n\\n**M5 — Validity controls:** per-frame potential energy (with the reference force-field evaluator) and minimum non-bonded contact distance. Ensembles that fail these are excluded from dynamics scoring rather than silently affecting TACF/PSD.\\n\\n**M6 — Scalar discrepancies (the adjudicating numbers):** for each dihedral, RMSE and relative-L2 between generated C(τ) and (i) full reference C0(τ) and (ii) the respective subsampled reference C_k(τ); likewise for PSD over the fast-mode frequency band. Aggregated over dihedrals with bootstrap 95% confidence intervals across the G trajectories × seeds.\\n\\n### Analysis logic — the two interpretable outcomes\\n- **Outcome (a) — faithful reproduction, no extrapolation (\\\"no\\\"):** MDGEN(sub_k)'s C(τ)/PSD/τ_int are statistically indistinguishable from R_k's (within M6 tolerance) but are separated from R0's fast modes (τ_int too large, high-frequency PSD peaks absent/flattened). Conclusion: MDGEN recovers the subsampled statistics; it does **not** recover the fast dynamics missing from subsampled MD.\\n- **Outcome (b) — recovery beyond the training signal (\\\"yes\\\"):** MDGEN(sub_k)'s fast-band PSD peaks and τ_int match R0's within tolerance even though the training data did not contain those modes. Conclusion: MDGEN regenerates fast torsional oscillations beyond its available signal.\\n- **Intermediate:** MDGEN(sub_k) is between R_k and R0 (partial peak amplitude). Reported quantitatively as the fraction of reference fast-band PSD amplitude recovered, not forced into yes/no.\\n\\nMDGEN(FULL) is the positive control: its generated ensemble is compared to R0 and must reproduce fast modes (validating that the pipeline and metrics can detect fast dynamics when the information is present).\\n\\n### Pre-registered \\\"accurate recovery\\\" criterion (directional, chosen before running)\\n\\\"Accurate recovery\\\" is defined as the conjunction, per condition:\\n1. **PSD peak recovery:** for fast-band frequencies f > f_nyq(k), the generated ensemble's mean PSD amplitude is ≥ 50% of the full reference peak amplitude at those frequencies, with the bootstrap 95% CI including ≥ 50% (i.e., we can detect recovery, not just nonzero amplitude); **and**\\n2. **TACF fidelity:** relative-L2 between generated and full-reference C(τ) ≤ 0.15 (aggregated over dihedrals), with the CI upper bound ≤ 0.15; **and**\\n3. **Equilibrium/validity intact:** P(φ), RDFs, energy/clash checks pass (§5 controls).\\n\\nJustification of tolerances: 50% PSD-peak amplitude is chosen as the level at which the generated signal's oscillatory power is physically meaningful (an order-of-magnitude above typical flat-noise power in a badly smoothed spectrum), and 0.15 relative-L2 corresponds to a visible but not over-tight fit, keeping the test sensitive to failure while not being trivially failed by sampling noise (sampling noise estimated from seed scatter and incorporated into the CIs). A condition failing any clause yields a directional \\\"no\\\" for that subsample level. These numbers are fixed at design time and are not retrofitted to results.\\n\\n### Statistical rigor\\nG independent generated trajectories per condition and ≥ 5 seeds; bootstrap resampling over (trajectory, seed) for all CIs and discrepancy metrics; τ_int and peak-amplitude distributions compared with a two-sided test at α = 0.05 with the pre-registered effect direction. Nyquist check (§controls) is a prerequisite gate before interpreting any fast-band metric.\\n\\n## 6. Feasibility\\n**Constraints verified in the sandbox:** no GPU; 224 CPUs available; network disabled; system Python 3.14.4 with numpy/scipy/matplotlib/mdtraj/MDAnalysis all missing; no data or MDGEN code present. The task note states repositories and compute are not required, so the design itself is the deliverable; the following paths make execution feasible in principle when external inputs are supplied.\\n\\n- **Inference-first path (primary):** rely on official pretrained MDGEN checkpoints for the reference system; then no training is needed. Cost: 8 conditions (FULL + 7 subsampled; or fewest per fallback) × G generations of length L each, plus metric computation. This is the only path that is GPU-feasible at scale and is CPU-runnable for the (small) generation + analysis load.\\n- **Training-required path (fallback):** if pretrained checkpoints are unavailable, reduce the sweep to the minimal decisive contrast: MDGEN(FULL) vs. MDGEN(sub_1ps) vs. MDGEN(sub_10ps), with G reduced (e.g., 50) and a single molecule. This preserves the central contrast and both outcome interpretations while making CPU-only training tractable on 224 cores.\\n- **Analysis tooling:** pinned requirements (numpy, scipy, matplotlib, and, if the reference format requires it, mdtraj/MDAnalysis) are installed at execution time; none are relied upon from this sandbox.\\n\\n## 7. Assumptions (explicit — not facts)\\n1. MDGEN refers to the published MD-trajectory generative model; its code and official pretrained checkpoints are obtained externally (network is disabled in this sandbox, and no such artifacts are present in /workspace).\\n2. The high-frequency reference is an all-atom MD17/TrajectoryNet-style trajectory of a single drug-like molecule at 0.5 fs, ~50–100 ps, vacuum, defined force field; this determines the dihedral set and the fast-mode frequency band.\\n3. \\\"Fast dynamics\\\" is operationalized as torsion-mode periods below the relevant subsample Nyquist, detected via TACF/PSD; equivalence to the source paper's \\\"torsion angle autocorrelation\\\" framing is assumed.\\n4. \\\"Accurate recovery\\\" means the pre-registered criterion in §5 (50% PSD-peak recovery and relative-L2 ≤ 0.15), decided before running.\\n5. The empirical polarity (outcome (a) vs. (b)) is an open question the design adjudicates; no prior is assumed.\\n6. Runtime budget is CPU-only; the inference-first path is default, with the defined fallback in §6 if checkpoints are unavailable.\\n\\n## 8. Uncertainties\\n- Exact MDGEN interface/conditioning and checkpoint availability are unverified in the sandbox; they pin down data-format and decoding-step details only at execution time.\\n- Whether the reference system's fast-mode band is resolvable at 0.5 fs must be confirmed by the Nyquist gate; if not, the assumption set in §7 is revisited.\\n- Statistical tolerances (50%, 0.15) are judgment calls justified in §5 but not empirically calibrated here.\\n\\n## 9. Controls (summary)\\n- **Nyquist gate:** verify reference fast-band PSD peaks exist above each R_k's f_nyq before claiming those modes are missing; else that subsample level is uninformative and excluded.\\n- **Positive control:** MDGEN(FULL) must reproduce R0's fast modes, validating metric sensitivity.\\n- **Interpolation baseline:** linear interpolation (torsion/Cartesian) of each R_k — lower bound on what any method can recover from subsampled information alone; MDGEN must beat it to claim any extrapolation.\\n- **Equilibrium-static control:** P(φ) and RDFs match under all conditions (§5 M4), isolating temporal-dynamics failures from structural degradation.\\n- **Validity control:** energy/clash checks (§5 M5).\\n- **Statistical:** G trajectories × ≥5 seeds, bootstrap CIs, α = 0.05 directional tests.\", \"evidence\": \"Sandbox inspection (all executed): /workspace contains only task.json whose documents.track.md reads \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"; `find /workspace -type f` and `/tmp`/`/` scans found no MDGEN code, checkpoints, trajectories, notebooks, or docs; Python 3.14.4 with numpy/scipy/matplotlib/mdtraj/MDAnalysis all raising ImportError; network disabled (socket connect to pypi.org:443 fails); no GPU; 224 CPUs. These verified constraints directly justify the feasibility section (inference-first, CPU-only fallback, pinned-dependency install at execution time) and the explicit-assumption treatment of external inputs.\", \"assumptions\": [\"MDGEN is the published MD-trajectory generative model; code and official pretrained checkpoints are obtained externally (not present in the sandbox; network disabled).\", \"Reference data is an all-atom MD17/TrajectoryNet-style single molecule at 0.5 fs, ~50-100 ps, vacuum, defined force field, serving as full-resolution ground truth.\", \"'Fast dynamics' = torsion-mode frequencies above the subsample Nyquist, measured via TACF and PSD.\", \"'Accurate recovery' = the pre-registered criterion (≥50% of full-reference fast-band PSD peak amplitude recovered, relative-L2 between generated and full-reference C(tau) <= 0.15, with equilibrium/validity intact).\", \"Empirical polarity (faithful alias-flattened reproduction vs. recovery beyond training signal) is an open question the design adjudicates.\", \"Runtime budget is CPU-only; inference-first via pretrained checkpoints is the default, with a defined reduced-sweep fallback if training is required.\", \"No experiment was run and no outcome is claimed; only the design is delivered.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Exact MDGEN interface/conditioning, trajectory file format, and checkpoint availability are unverified in the sandbox and pin down details only at execution time.\", \"Whether the reference system's fast-mode band is resolvable at 0.5 fs must pass the pre-registered Nyquist gate; otherwise that subsample level is excluded as uninformative.\", \"Pre-registered tolerances (50% PSD-peak recovery; relative-L2 0.15) are design judgments with stated justification, not empirically calibrated.\", \"Per-dihedral weighting (methyl vs. heavy torsions) and the exact PSD windowing/τ-cutoff must be locked at execution time from the reference topology and expected correlation times.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-5c97d9bd7066451aa6fa19a024f50fd2", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": null, "kind": "ActionEvent"}
